SM(473,{"n":"Acts 17 Conclusion","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Acts 17 Conclusion.mp3","d":2259.2,"s":[[0.48,"Come and study Holy Scripture with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.48,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed tradition, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[23.28,"And they took him and brought him to the Areopagus, saying, May we know what this new drop doctrine is of which you speak."],[31.68,"So they these philosophers, they get they're they're curious at least."],[40.72,"So they bring him over."],[43.76,"And uh you are bringing some strange things to our ears."],[48.24,"Therefore, we want to know what these things mean."],[50.64,"Okay, so they're at least curious."],[53.76,"What is this strange stuff you're talking about?"],[56.48,"For all the Athenians and the foreigners who were there spent their time in nothing else but either to tell or to hear some new thing."],[63.64,"So it's a little judgmental from St."],[65.4,"Luke. But there are a lot of folks who just like hanging out in the marketplace and talking about stuff."],[72.04,"So it is kind of like an internet message board."],[78.6,"Then Paul stood in the midst of the Areopagus and said, Men of Athens, I perceive that in all things you are very religious."],[87.4,"For as I was passing through and considering the objects of your worship, I even found an altar with this inscription to the unknown God."],[96.22,"So he starts out, he says, you know, they've picked up on the fact that he's talking about religious ideas."],[101.42,"He says, Well, you folks are really religious."],[103.98,"You got a lot of temples, a lot of idols here"],[107.59,"And he says, while I was walking around, I even saw this one, this one said, to the unknown God."],[115.47,"Meaning, just whichever ones we don't know about."],[120.11,"There's somebody out there."],[121.39,"Let's make sure we cover all our bases."],[125.07,"So just whoever we know, we'll give them some sacrifices too."],[128.91,"Can't help hurt to have them in our corner."],[132.51,"So he says, I notice this, you know."],[134.19,"So you're you're you know, you're very you're very religious."],[138.59,"Therefore, the one whom you worship without knowing him, I proclaim to you."],[145.07,"So what he's saying there is."],[148.44,"That the the God you don't know about."],[152.42,"I'm here to tell you about the God you don't know about."],[156.42,"You've got all these other gods, but I'm here to tell you about the world."],[159.62,"So, well, oh, okay."],[161.22,"Well, this is a new thing."],[162.6,"So he's starting out on their level."],[168.52,"He says, God who made the world and everything in it, since he is Lord of heaven and earth, does not dwell in temples made with hands."],[177.8,"So again, this is a very good philosophical idea."],[183.96,"Since Plato, uh the Euthyphro is the dialogue to look at about this, where he he talks about the gods."],[191.16,"But the more philosophically minded Greeks had looked at sort of Greek mythology and the way the gods behaved."],[198.48,"Greek mythology, they kind of said, you know, gods like that aren't really worth worshiping."],[206.8,"So a lot of them had sort of taken the approach that, well, you know, that mythology stuff is for the uneducated people."],[215.12,"You know, to try to explain some of these philosophical ideas to them because they don't get them."],[220.64,"You know, but we as philosophers, we we know that, you know, the divine obviously is not behave like that and not do these kind of things."],[229.92,"So what St."],[230.8,"Paul says is this is something they would agree with."],[234.06,"They'd say, well, if there's a God who made the universe, well, yeah, he's not a concept of the divine that's behind everything, that made everything, especially the Stoics."],[245.38,"Yeah. It's a little bit like the force."],[249.62,"It's kind of impersonal, but it's it's this force that is the real religious."],[257.14,"But this is something they would have agreed with."],[258.82,"Yeah. Yeah. The real God, the real divine, does not need you to bring them food."],[264.21,"The philosoph the philosophical text would say, okay, yeah, well, yeah."],[269.38,"So you're not coming to tell us about some folk religion."],[272.58,"Okay, that's good."],[273.54,"We'll keep listening."],[278.03,"Nor is he worshipped with men's hands as though he needed anything, since he gives to all life, breath, and all things"],[285.49,"So obviously, if he's the one who makes people alive, and he's the one who gives everything to people, he doesn't need people to give him anything."],[292.3,"Again, this is okay."],[294.06,"Yeah. They could this is uh uh an idea they could agree with."],[298.46,"And he has made from one blood every nation of men to dwell on all the face of the earth, and has determined their preappointed times and the boundaries of their dwellings."],[308.94,"So notice. Stoics are really on board with this."],[312.14,"Yeah. But but notice um he doesn't go back to."],[318.24,"When he's talking to the Jews, he goes back to Abraham, he talks about our forefathers."],[322.54,"He doesn't try to catch these philosophers up with the Old Testament."],[327.44,"Other than Genesis."],[328.64,"He goes back to Genesis."],[329.68,"He goes, he's going back to the Tower of Babel."],[332.1,"When the people were scattered and God established their boundaries."],[335.14,"You know, Deuteronomy 32, when it talks about the Tower of Babel, uses that language of establishing the boundaries of the places where they would live."],[342.3,"So he goes back to these pagan Athenians, their connection to God, which goes back to that."],[348.22,"Because since then they've kind of wandered off astray."],[352.46,"But you go back there, you go back there, and eventually you get back to where all humanity was one, created by God, and then God scattered them and appointed them these different places."],[366.98,"And yes, in both the Stoic and in Plato's Timaeus."],[371.56,"There's sort of original people, you know, from whom all people are descended."],[379.48,"So that they should seek the Lord in the hope that they might grope for him and find him, though he is not far from each one of us."],[389.16,"So this is also an idea that the Stoics and Epicureans could understand."],[393.5,"Because the Stoics believe well, if you come to the logos of anything, that's ultimately connected back to the beginning."],[401.42,"But notice also how St."],[403.5,"Paul understands what's happened to the Gentile nations."],[408.14,"Paul doesn't understand it as well, God rejected all of you and chose Israel"],[409.22,"St. That's not his understanding."],[418.21,"His understanding is that when the people were scattered at the Tower of Babel, the intent was that a day would come, which is the day Saint Paul's now living in."],[428.3,"When they would be brought back."],[431.82,"And that the truth is, even when they were out scattered there, even when they were worshiping these false gods, even while there's a preservational powers, God was not far away."],[444.14,"And as it says, God was hoping that they would, you know, grope is an interesting word because it implies, you know, sort of like a blind person feeling.ing in the darkness, you know, not able to see exactly where he is, but just kind of reach out for him"],[458.42,"And this is where the idea comes from that you find in the church fathers and and in other later Orthodox writers of the idea that every human being who is in the world has access to some part of the truth, even if it's very small."],[472.02,"By living in God's creation, which is filled with the glory of God and with and with Christ, that everyone has access to some little peace."],[482.58,"And so salvation for every person depends on what they do with the truth that they've been given."],[489.38,"And that often, if what we do with the truth we've been given is honoring to God, then we're given more."],[496.74,"We saw that already in the book of Acts with Cornelius."],[500.46,"Cornelius is a centurion, he's at the fringes of the synagogue community."],[505.1,"You know, he can't really convert and become Jewish."],[508.94,"But he's worshiping only the God of Israel."],[512.62,"He stopped worshiping other gods."],[514.14,"He's only worshiping the God of Israel."],[515.42,"He's giving alms, he's supporting the synagogue."],[517.9,"He's doing all of these things, and what happens?"],[521.42,"God brings St."],[523.74,"Peter, you know, to preach the gospel to him."],[526.78,"You know, because of what he's done with what he has."],[530.86,"And so St."],[532.3,"Paul is here saying that even though the nations were all dispersed, and yes, God chose Israel to reveal himself, he gave them more."],[541.95,"That they were not completely cut off and left orphaned."],[546.95,"God was still there."],[547.99,"God was still sending them the rain in its season."],[551.03,"God was still providing them with life and with these other things."],[554.55,"So that they might, you know, with what little they had, start to, you know, grope their way back to God Himself."],[565.67,"For in him we live and move and have our being, as also some of your own poetic poet."],[570.79,"But it is"],[576.95,"And what St."],[578.07,"Paul is quoting here, hold on to your chair."],[581.75,"What he's quoting here is a hymn to Zeus."],[584.95,"He quotes a hymn to Zeus"],[589.79,"Well, why would he do that?"],[590.91,"Is he saying Zeus and Yahweh, the God of Israel are the same person?"],[594.43,"No. He could have said that."],[597.11,"When he started, remember?"],[598.23,"When he started, he didn't say, you know, you're worshiping Zeus, but let me tell you who Zeus really is."],[603.88,"That's not what he's saying."],[604.76,"That would have been perfectly acceptable to them."],[606.6,"To them, yeah."],[607.8,"But no, he chose this unknown God."],[610.44,"So he's not saying the God of Zeus are the same person."],[613.08,"But what he's saying is these things that you've been saying about these other spirits, these other things that aren't gods, really, are not true about them, but they are true about the one actual true God."],[631.25,"So he says, you kind of have this understanding of God that he's close to us."],[637.59,"That he's nearby."],[640.07,"And that would conform with the Stoics, because the Stoics, I would imagine, would see this, yes, if there was a divine God, it would appear that coming into God would be like this."],[651.65,"And as near as we can tell, they're all tracking with him still so far."],[655.91,"They're still open, at least open to this."],[658.25,"Okay, okay. For we are also his offspring."],[668.42,"Therefore, since we are the offspring of God, we ought not to think that the divine nature is like gold or silver or stone, something shaped by art and man's devising."],[679.64,"So he's coming back to that idea."],[682.49,"God created us, to be his children."],[685.23,"And that's what he wants."],[686.35,"He wants for us to come back and be his children."],[689.92,"But, you know, that means that God cannot be sort of crafted."],[697.29,"And remember, the way idolatry worked was it wasn't just that, oh, I make this thing and now I think it's a God."],[703.99,"But they believed that once they crafted this idol, that the God would come and dwell in it."],[708.31,"And then through that, through the way they manipulated the idol and the way they they did the rituals and everything, they could sort of make the god do what wanted he, she, it, to do."],[723.7,"And so he's not just attacking the idea that, well, yeah, you can't make a god out of a rock."],[730.03,"But he's saying, you know, if this is who God is, then you can't come and manipulate him."],[739.24,"And use him and get him to do what you want him to do."],[743.32,"Because it implied in a God is sovereignty, and that's not true."],[747.78,"Well, yeah, and that's and that's the the fatherhood image he's using."],[751.06,"Yeah. Because remember, you're you're the Potter Familius."],[754.22,"The head of the household, the father of the Roman family, wasn't subject to what the children wanted."],[763.08,"Some families today, maybe, you know, the children, you know, get a vote, but not in the Roman world."],[769.85,"So it's the same kind of idea."],[772.35,"If God is really our father, if this is who God is, then we can't sit here and do a magical ritual or do this or that in this temple and get him to do what we want."],[782.69,"He tells us what he wants us to do, and we that would need to be obedient."],[790.05,"Truly, these times of ignorance, God overlooked, but now commands all men everywhere to repent."],[797.17,"So this is the second piece."],[798.73,"Not only was there some little bit of knowledge of God there that you could have grasped after."],[804.47,"But because of this period of ignorance, God has overlooked some of these sins."],[811.88,"St. Paul's gonna say in Romans that where there is no law, sins are not reckoned."],[818.4,"So, you know, God chose to reveal the Torah, to reveal the law to Israel."],[825.36,"And as soon as he did, Israel became accountable to that."],[830.17,"In all its details."],[831.87,"Once it was once it was given to them."],[833.95,"He didn't give that to the Athenians."],[838.13,"And since he didn't give it to the Athenians, they weren't they were accountable for what they had."],[842.97,"With what they had."],[843.79,"Not for all the things they didn't have."],[845.72,"But St. Paul is saying, now."],[847.82,"Now what's happening?"],[848.94,"Well, St. Paul's bringing the gospel to these people."],[852.22,"These people are coming to Christ."],[853.66,"These people are being called back starting with Pentecost."],[856.3,"And so now they are going to be accountable."],[858.54,"And so now they need to repent to return to God."],[862.54,"And they are going to be held responsible now for what they do in response to the gospel."],[868.14,"Because that's been given to everyone."],[873.02,"Well, repent literally means to change your mind, the Greek word."],[878.06,"Metania. Okay So they may have understood it in a very intellectu.alized fashion of turning from error to truth"],[891.86,"But that's not totally off."],[894.34,"Yeah. So they wouldn't have had a perfect understanding of what he meant in the moral dimension."],[902.04,"But they would have had that amount of it."],[905.82,"You know, before it was okay, not okay, but before, you know, you went and you did these things religiously and this kind of thing and honored these other gods."],[914.6,"And God has continued to bless you in all of these ways, despite that."],[918.77,"But now that you're hearing the truth, you're responsible to believe and follow the truth."],[926.01,"You know, and if you turn back to these other things now, God's not going to overlook it."],[932.39,"Or excuse it."],[936.63,"Because he has appointed a day on which he will judge the world in righteousness by the man whom he has ordained."],[943.11,"He has given assurance of this to all by raising him from the dead."],[948.23,"Okay? So he concludes it by getting to Jesus."],[951.59,"He says, What?"],[953.43,"There's a time coming when everyone is going to be judged and have to give an account."],[958.07,"That's why you need to repent."],[960.12,"And he's shown us that this man, Jesus, is the one who's going to be the judge, the one to whom we're going to have to give account."],[968.84,"He did that by raising him from the dead."],[969.48,"So he's explained now there"],[975.76,"Remember their original question: who's Jesus?"],[978.08,"Who's Anostasis? And now he said, Jesus is this man who's returning to judge the dead."],[987.2,"And Anostasis is him being raised from the dead."],[990.32,"That was our symbol that this is true."],[994.58,"Our symbol that this is true."],[999.54,"We sometimes leave out when we talk about the gospel."],[1005.48,"When we talk about the gospel, uh, just in the in America, because we've been conditioned by Protestantism."],[1012.48,"We think of telling people how to get saved."],[1015.65,"That that's what the gospel is."],[1017.49,"Biblically, that's not what the gospel is."],[1020.29,"Biblically, the gospel is what Jesus did."],[1023.97,"That's the good news."],[1025.09,"The good news is here's what Jesus did."],[1028.15,"And then you notice when when the gospel's preaching in Acts, we've seen this over and over again, they talk about who Jesus is and what he did, and then the people's response is, what must I do to be saved?"],[1041.0,"Because I believe this is true, now what do I need to do?"],[1045.04,"But the gospel is what is what Jesus did."],[1049.44,"And if you if you really narrow in on it, when they ask, what must I do to be saved, what are they talking about being saved from because the preaching we've seen in the book of Acts has not been Saint Paul coming and running down a list of sins do you do this do you do you drink do you smoke do you dance the hoochie cooch right do you do that you need to repent get right get saved right that's not how Saint Paul is preaching right so it's not talking about being saved from the sins you've committed it's it's death well it's saved from death well and what's what's the conclusion when we run down the list of what Christ has done?"],[1090.56,"What's the last thing we say?"],[1093.44,"That Jesus is returning to judge the living and the dead."],[1097.97,"Which includes wrath."],[1099.21,"I don't want to limit it to wrath."],[1100.69,"Because Jesus returning to judge the living and the dead is also bringing a reward for those who have been faithful and for the righteous."],[1113.25,"But Christ is returning to judge the living and the dead."],[1116.21,"So if someone tells you everything Christ has done."],[1120.15,"How he's rejected, he was crucified, he rose again, he ascended into heaven where he's enthroned and he's ruling over the whole creation."],[1127.59,"And the time is going to come when he's going to return and he's going to judge the living dead, including you."],[1134.47,"What's the next question?"],[1136.87,"What must I do to be saved?"],[1138.63,"What do I need to do to prepare for that day when I stand before Christ and have to give an account? for who I am and what I've done in this life"],[1148.17,"And so that's why that's a major emphasis here."],[1151.69,"That's why that's where St."],[1153.77,"Paul, even though we're going to see that last little bit is what throws them all."],[1159.9,"That's where he has to end up because that's the at the core of what the gospel is."],[1165.36,"That Jesus has been raised from the dead."],[1167.36,"Now he's coming to judge living the dead."],[1169.44,"And because of that, you need to repent and prepare, you know, to stand before him and give an account."],[1176.48,"It's not living your new up your new life now."],[1178.89,"It's not negotiable."],[1179.85,"You can't just leave off with the, you know, God loves us all and he's close to all of us."],[1185.29,"And, you know, we're all. his children and all that stuff where they were nodding"],[1191.41,"It's interesting because he goes on for quite a long sermon without saying anything that they can't wrap their minds around and and more or less accept."],[1203.97,"Yeah. And then you come to the actual historical event of Christ's life and death and resurrection."],[1214.77,"And that's outside of their prepared ideas."],[1219.01,"And that's not totally dissimilar from the other sermons we've seen him preach."],[1223.41,"Just those were in a Jewish context."],[1225.25,"And you notice how he started those."],[1226.77,"He would start those with Abraham."],[1228.05,"Abraham, our father, was brought from the Galilee."],[1234.41,"Yeah. You know, and the law was given to Moses."],[1238.97,"Yeah, Moses, yeah."],[1240.65,"You know, and then he'd get to the part where Jesus has come as he's the Messiah, and he was crucified."],[1246.25,"And they all go, huh?"],[1249.45,"When he gets to that end part."],[1252.89,"Yeah, it's the same kind of thing."],[1254.25,"He gets to that, and that's where things sort of derail for them."],[1259.91,"But so that's that's sort of St."],[1261.99,"Paul's preaching method is to bring them on board."],[1266.13,"You agree with me about that?"],[1267.17,"You agree with me about this, and then take that through to its conclusion."],[1270.45,"Well, if you agree with me about all these other things I said."],[1273.27,"Well, then here's the natural conclusion."],[1276.09,"And so we've seen a lot of people who get to that natural conclusion, and or like the Bereans, go and go to the scriptures and say, Well, yeah, that is where this is all going."],[1286.98,"And agree and accept and become Christians."],[1289.78,"And then other people get to that last part and jump off board."],[1295.44,"And so that's what we're about to see here."],[1296.88,"It's that same kind of method."],[1299.04,"It's a little different."],[1300.16,"He's going back further."],[1302.32,"He's going back to the Tower of Babel, and he's going back to, you know, when the Gentiles and Jews were one, when Gumanians one, and walking them through it."],[1311.68,"But yeah, eventually you get to that point where talk about the resurrection of the dead and Christ returning to judge the living and the dead, and we're about to see their response."],[1318.8,"of the dead. When they heard of the resurrection, some mocked"],[1327.88,"So a group of them not only reject it, but they think it's ridiculous."],[1333.8,"And start making fun of it."],[1335.72,"Now, why would that be?"],[1337.08,"Well, you know, you go back to Plato, the body is the prison house of the soul."],[1342.78,"The meat, the flesh."],[1344.06,"And this is something Stoics and Epicureans."],[1345.98,"Remember, the Stoics don't care about the particulars, and they want to get to the the universals and the you know the things behind it."],[1353.44,"Well, your body, the fact that you get tired, the fact that you have to eat, the fact that you have to go to the bathroom, the fact that they have that's a hindrance."],[1360.42,"They want like pure cut."],[1362.1,"So why after you are dead and your soul is set free, would you want to come back to life?"],[1362.66,"That's absurd Okay."],[1369.12,"And and the Epicureas, as we said, aren't focused on sort of these lower level press, it's not these sort of intellectual pleasure, the life of the intellect."],[1376.4,"Well, why would you possibly you know, perhaps if your soul is set free, you can engage in pure contemplation, you know, without ceasing."],[1387.68,"Yeah. So so to them, the idea that someone would want even want to come back to life in a bot is just absurd."],[1396.42,"And so that's why they're just what?"],[1398.74,"You know, like what do you?"],[1400.22,"This is the way a a lot of of contemporary Christians think is that you you you die and escape from your and are off in heaven, and the resurrection of the body, which is got lost somewhere to Christianity, is just lost someplace."],[1419.26,"Yeah, yeah. One bright morning when this life is over, I'll fly away."],[1422.78,"Yeah. Yeah, and so that's that's a real place."],[1430.78,"There's a great there's a great famous quote from N."],[1432.94,"T. Wright, who's the bishop who was the Anglican Bishop of Durham."],[1436.7,"He's retired now, but he's a New Testament scholar."],[1438.86,"He said he doesn't find life after death very interesting."],[1442.14,"He's interested in life after life after death."],[1447.02,"What happens to you when you die?"],[1448.78,"It's the new heavens and the new earth."],[1450.38,"It's when Christ returns, when we're resurrected."],[1452.58,"That's the important part."],[1454.42,"That's the thrust, you know, of uh of what's important."],[1458.74,"Um, so yeah, there is a lot of that, you know, in and and and that's very common in non-Christian religions, too."],[1466.34,"I mean, that's at the core of Buddhism."],[1468.02,"Oh, yeah. Is escaping and Hinduism too, escaping this world and moving on to some purer form of existence of physical."],[1476.64,"Or non existence in the case of Buddhism."],[1483.52,"Yeah, to get free."],[1484.88,"In Hinduism, it's a wheel that you're trapped in that you're trying to escape."],[1488.96,"Okay. Um, you know, Buddhism, you've got the veil of tears, you know, and you're basically trying to cease to exist because existence is suffering."],[1496.0,"But that's very con and the same kind of thing here"],[1497.8,"So you're trying to, you know, get past it."],[1502.44,"You're wanting to get out of this world and out of this body and away from all this."],[1506.84,"And so Christianity takes a radically different position to that."],[1512.2,"That God is redeeming, not He's not redeeming us out of the world, He's redeeming the world."],[1517.4,"He's redeeming his creation, all of it."],[1520.92,"You know, and and and bringing it back to Himself."],[1527.0,"And Protestantism, it's very right."],[1529.76,"It' as very Protestant way of looking at things."],[1532.36,"Yeah. I never thought of it that way."],[1534.92,"Well, I was raised with that so strongly."],[1537.72,"It's like once we're we're going to heaven, yeah."],[1539.52,"Yeah, we're going to heaven, we're not going to have bodies anymore."],[1541.0,"Whatever Yeah. Set out a cloud with a harp and uh whatever."],[1548.08,"And it's found, it's found the idea."],[1550.96,"I was raised with the idea that the body was pretty bad."],[1555.68,"It's one of those things that tempts you to sin all the time."],[1557.84,"Yeah. It leads you into sin and uh it's uh um you know it's it's only physical, yeah."],[1568.06,"Uh whereas things that matter are spiritual."],[1571.82,"I mean this is yeah, this is actually ancient, ancient Greek."],[1576.3,"The Greeks would have said mental instead of spiritual, but say not that dissimilar."],[1581.86,"Yeah, but that's that's the Protestant faith that I was told."],[1586.3,"And you you find that in at least a lot of medieval Roman Catholicism, too."],[1592.22,"Like you read Thomas Aquinas, uh, and of course he doesn't deny the resurrection of the body."],[1599.5,"He talks about it."],[1600.7,"But before he talks about the resurrection of the body, he spends a great deal of time talking about the beatific vision."],[1606.46,"Where once we're outside of the body, now we can behold and gaze upon God."],[1614.86,"Well, that's it."],[1615.42,"There's sort of it's never sort of how how do those two ideas work together is never sort of explained."],[1620.78,"It's like, well, wait, if I'm free from the body and I'm having this incredible vision of God, and I'll why would I want to be so there's there a's a t a tension there too uh in in Roman Catholicism, and and uh Protestantism is in lot of cases has gone way in that direction too."],[1639.38,"But to what degree do you think uh the Gnostic heresies end in the I don't think directly."],[1645.38,"Okay, I mean I think it it uh I think it is a sort of Gnostic eschatology that has crept in."],[1651.86,"I well, I think in the case of like Thomas Aquinas, it's it's Platonism, it's it's philosophy."],[1657.86,"It's gotten in there through philosophy, through Greek philosophy."],[1661.46,"Um I think in the case of that just sort of Protestant view where you go to heaven or you go to hell, and either way, your body I guess that's just gaps in the reconstruction forget about it."],[1672.9,"That is basically a Gnostic exitch esatology. but I don't think it came from anybody reading the Gnostic Gospels or anything"],[1681.5,"Trying to piece together Christianity."],[1683.42,"Yeah, there's a there's a really interesting book uh by Harold Bloom, who I think is now dead."],[1690.22,"Um but he was the professor of Shakespeare at Yale for decades."],[1697.9,"And he wrote a book called The American Religion."],[1702.62,"And he's talks about sort of the whole swath of religious experience, especially coming out in 19th and early 20th century America."],[1712.78,"And he argues that there is sort of one American religion."],[1715.98,"He says the two best examples of it are the Southern Baptist Convention and the Mormons."],[1722.58,"And he argues that that religion is not really Christianity."],[1728.18,"Well, no, it's not not about it."],[1729.62,"He says it's a form of Gnosticism."],[1731.54,"He argues it's a form of Gnosticism."],[1734.5,"That it's about denying the world, in terms of morals and ethics."],[1739.4,"That's one of the things he's getting at with the Mormons and the Southern Baptists."],[1742.6,"You know, that any of these sort of pleasurable things, food or drink or you know, tobacco or alcohol or any of these things, or all, yeah, any of those sort of pleasurable things of this world are sort of evil, you know, and pushed away."],[1758.92,"And yeah, everything's about going to heaven, you know, when you die and leaving this body and this world behind."],[1765.28,"But it's an interesting book."],[1766.72,"But he he talks about that and shows though that those threads that have more in common with Gnosticism than Christianity are sort of woven through a lot of coming out of the second great awakening in the 19th century, woven through a lot of American, particularly US American religious experience."],[1787.36,"But yeah, so that's something you could and Harold Bloom himself considered himself to be a Gnostic."],[1793.84,"Oh, he did."],[1794.8,"Yes. So he saw Gnostic elements in those."],[1800.96,"Yeah, and so he had done a great deal of reading in the Gnostic Gospels and all those texts."],[1805.44,"And so that's where he's getting the connections he's making."],[1808.24,"Wow. Where he's saying, you know, this seems a lot more like this thing than like the Bible telling."],[1814.44,"So a group of them they mock him."],[1816.2,"Just outright mock him."],[1817.64,"But then another, while others said, We will hear you again on this on this matter."],[1821.58,"So there's a few who are like, uh I'll hear a little more."],[1826.14,"You know. We don't know if that's because they're thinking of converting, or if, you know, as St."],[1832.54,"Luke said, the Athenians just really like hearing new things."],[1836.22,"You know, and this is curious."],[1840.22,"Yeah. Um so Paul departed from among them."],[1844.38,"However, some men joined him and believed among them Dionysius the Areopagite, a woman named Demaris, and others with them."],[1852.94,"So traditionally, Saint Dionysius the Areopagite ended up becoming the first bishop of Athens, the first overseer of the church in Athens as the Christian community starts to form."],[1867.86,"And backing that up is the fact that he's so prominently mentioned here."],[1875.28,"That, you know, why would you know he he names two people out of this whole group?"],[1880.64,"Okay. Dionysus Areagite and Domeris."],[1884.14,"So clearly, minimally, this de Dionysius Areopagite was a prominent member of the early Christian community."],[1893.74,"And so it makes very logical sense that he was the first one in that overseer role. as the bishop"],[1901.78,"Now, before we end a brief, very brief, I'll try to keep it brief."],[1907.06,"Exorcist on there are a series of works written by Dionysius the Areopagite, attributed to him."],[1918.34,"These were believed for most of Christian history to have been written by this Saint Deodysius the Areopagite."],[1932.82,"Most scholars now reject that idea."],[1938.3,"For several reasons."],[1939.94,"The most glaring one being nobody seems to have heard. of them or read them before the 5th or 6th century"],[1947.98,"In the 6th century, you start finding people quoting them all of a sudden."],[1952.46,"But nobody in the 5th century, nobody in the 3rd century."],[1955.55,"If these were all written in the first century, you'd think someone somewhere would have heard of them."],[1960.43,"Right by that."],[1962.03,"Now, we don't have, we have a tiny fraction of what was written between the first century and the sixth century."],[1970.03,"So we can't say for sure that no one ever mentioned them."],[1973.95,"We can just say in the stuff we have, nobody mentions them."],[1978.99,"So that's not clear."],[1980.67,"If you look at most books about him and about these works, they will say that they believe they were written in the sixth century. in Syria"],[1991.83,"One prominent scholar of these works had one of the greatest lines ever, which is there are two things of which we can be certain about these writings of St."],[2002.39,"Deus Areapagai. One is that they were not written in the 6th century, and the other is that they were not written in Syria."],[2012.95,"So he said the only things we know for sure is that that's wrong."],[2017.01,"But really we don't know when and where they were written."],[2019.25,"Yeah. So there is there's a great deal of ambiguity."],[2024.21,"But they're very influential."],[2026.05,"They're very important works."],[2029.73,"And from the 6th century on. they're everywhere, and they're considered very authoritative"],[2036.65,"They're very important works, both in the East and in the West."],[2042.81,"So even though we will likely never be able to prove if and how they're connected to this Saint Dionysius."],[2054.81,"Or whether they were written by some later saint who was named after him."],[2060.39,"Which would be the other the other option."],[2067.27,"Yeah. Yeah, until until the resurrection, then we may find out."],[2071.87,"But until that point, we will probably never know for sure."],[2076.71,"Suffice it to say St."],[2077.43,"Those works are important works"],[2078.43,"Dinas is the Areopagite, the first bishop of Athens is an important saint."],[2089.01,"I think we've got the complete."],[2090.21,"Yeah. They're not they're not super long."],[2092.61,"Yeah. They're not super easy to read."],[2103.01,"So I mean they're very interesting, but it's kind of heavy stuff."],[2106.69,"It's not the kind of stuff where you just sort of read it like this."],[2109.49,"It's more you sort of read like a paragraph and kind of chew on it for a while, sort of think about it and you know."],[2117.01,"Yeah, read it again."],[2118.21,"Yeah, it's that kind of thing."],[2119.97,"Um I read I read it. it once and was kind of exhausting"],[2124.09,"Yeah, his his I mean some of his more famous probably his most well-known uh work out of that is called The Celestial Hierarchies, which is about the angels and the different orders of angels, the cherubim, the seraphim, thrones and dominions and powers and archangels."],[2142.17,"Yeah. So that that's probably not hard to understand."],[2145.85,"That part's not a good idea."],[2146.65,"Relatively, yeah. Um but yeah, that and then he wrote another work that's related to it called the Ecclesiastical Hierarchies, where he talks about bishops and priests and deacons and the way the church is structured, and tries to draw connections between the way sort of God's the angelic realm is structured and the way the church is structured."],[2166.49,"To show how how those are similar"],[2171.97,"Um but yeah, so this is the figure after whom they're they're named."],[2178.21,"They're named. That's um but we're not totally sure whether this is the person who wrote them or if it was a later saint who wrote them who was named after him."],[2192.93,"Okay. Um because obviously people just like today will take the names of the past. Dennis comes"],[2200.97,"Yeah, Dennis comes from Dionysius."],[2203.17,"Yeah. Oh, I didn't know that."],[2205.53,"Yeah, Son Denis and yeah."],[2208.97,"Is uh is from is from uh his name."],[2210.93,"from uh And uh Louis comes Elias, which is the prophet Elijah."],[2212.49,"It's a short name of his name."],[2218.89,"No, I never knew that really yeah in Greek it's Elias."],[2224.65,"Uh-huh. Okay. And the eh at the beginning gets dropped off."],[2227.87,"It becomes Louis."],[2233.71,"So thank you, everybody."],[2235.07,"Thank you. Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2244.11,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2249.47,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":23.28,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 17:19-21 - Brought to the Areopagus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Took him to the <b>Areopagus</b>: \"May we know what this <b>new doctrine</b> is?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">philosophers at least <b>curious</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"You are bringing some <b>strange things</b> to our ears\" - want to know what they mean</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Athenians and foreigners</b> spent time only to <b>tell or hear some new thing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"a little judgmental from St. Luke\"</li><li class=\"d1\">folks who like hanging out in the marketplace talking - \"kind of like an <b>internet message board</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":78.6,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 17:22-23 - The altar to the unknown God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>in the midst of the Areopagus</b>: \"Men of Athens, I perceive you are <b>very religious</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Found an altar: <b>\"To the unknown God\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= whichever ones we don't know about</li><li class=\"d1\">\"let's make sure we cover all our bases\" - sacrifices to whoever</li><li class=\"d1\">\"can't hurt to have them in our corner\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"The one whom you worship without knowing him, I proclaim to you\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= the God you don't know about - \"I'm here to tell you about\" him</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Starting on their level</b></li></ul>"},{"t":168.52,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 17:24-25 - God not in temples","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>God who made the world</b>, Lord of heaven and earth, <b>does not dwell in temples made with hands</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A good <b>philosophical idea</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Plato</b>, <i>Euthyphro</i> - on the gods</li><li class=\"d1\">philosophers had looked at <b>Greek mythology</b> and how gods behave: \"not really worth worshiping\"</li><li class=\"d1\">mythology = \"for the uneducated\" to explain ideas</li><li class=\"d1\">divine obviously doesn't behave like that</li><li class=\"d0\">Stoics: concept of the divine behind everything - \"a little bit like <b>the force</b>\", impersonal</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Nor is he worshiped with men's hands as though he needed anything</b> - he gives <b>life, breath, all things</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the real God <b>doesn't need food</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not folk religion - okay, we'll keep listening\"</li></ul>"},{"t":298.46,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 17:26 - One blood, boundaries set","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>From one blood every nation</b>, <b>preappointed times and boundaries</b> of their dwellings</li><li class=\"d1\">Stoics \"really on board\"</li><li class=\"d0\">To Jews: goes back to <b>Abraham</b>, \"our forefathers\"</li><li class=\"d0\">To these philosophers: back to <b>Genesis</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Tower of Babel</b> - people scattered, <b>boundaries</b> established</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Deuteronomy 32</b> uses the boundary language</li><li class=\"d0\">Their <b>connection to God</b> goes back there - since then \"wandered off astray\"</li><li class=\"d1\">back far enough: <b>all humanity one</b>, created by God, then scattered</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Stoic</b> and <b>Plato's <i>Timaeus</i></b>: original people all descended from</li></ul>"},{"t":379.48,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 17:27 - Groping for God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>That they should seek the Lord, grope for him and find him, though he is not far from each one of us</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Stoics/Epicureans can follow: <b>logos</b> connected back to the beginning</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's view of the <b>Gentile nations</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not</b> \"God rejected all of you and chose Israel\"</li><li class=\"d1\">at Babel the intent: a day would come - <b>the day Paul is living in</b> - when they'd be <b>brought back</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even scattered, worshiping false gods, under <b>principalities</b> - <b>God not far away</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Grope</b> = like a blind person feeling in the darkness, reaching out</li></ul>"},{"t":458.42,"lv":2,"h":"Access to truth; Cornelius","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Church fathers, later Orthodox: every human has <b>access to some part of the truth</b>, however small</li><li class=\"d1\">living in <b>creation</b>, filled with <b>God's glory</b> and <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Salvation depends on what they do with the truth they've been given</b></li><li class=\"d1\">honoring to God → <b>given more</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Cornelius</b> (Acts)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>centurion</b>, fringes of synagogue, can't fully convert</li><li class=\"d1\">worships <b>only the God of Israel</b>, <b>alms</b>, supports synagogue</li><li class=\"d1\">→ God brings <b>Peter</b> to preach to him</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: nations dispersed, God <b>chose Israel</b> to reveal himself and gave them more</li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>not cut off, orphaned</b> - still sending <b>rain in its season</b>, life</li><li class=\"d1\">with what little they had, <b>grope their way back</b></li></ul>"},{"t":565.67,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 17:28 - Quoting a hymn to Zeus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"In him we live and move and have our being\"</b> - as also some of your own poets</li><li class=\"d0\">Hold on to your chair: quoting a <b>hymn to Zeus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Zeus and Yahweh the <b>same person</b>? <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d1\">could have said \"you worship Zeus, let me tell you who Zeus really is\" - acceptable to them</li><li class=\"d1\">but he chose the <b>unknown God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">What he says: things claimed about <b>other spirits</b> (not really gods) are <b>not true of them</b>, but <b>true of the one true God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Stoics: God <b>close, nearby</b> - they're <b>still tracking</b> with him</li></ul>"},{"t":658.25,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 17:29 - Offspring of God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"We are also his offspring\"</b> → divine nature <b>not like gold, silver or stone</b>, shaped by art and man's devising</li><li class=\"d0\">Back to idea: God created us to be <b>his children</b> - wants us to <b>come back</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Idolatry mechanics</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"I make a thing and think it's a god\"</li><li class=\"d1\">god would <b>come dwell in</b> the crafted idol</li><li class=\"d1\">manipulate the idol with <b>rituals</b> → make the god do what he, she, it wants</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's point: if this is who God is, you <b>can't manipulate him</b></li><li class=\"d1\">manipulation implies he's <b>not sovereign</b></li></ul>"},{"t":747.78,"lv":3,"h":"The fatherhood image","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Father</b> image - <b>paterfamilias</b></li><li class=\"d1\">head of the Roman household <b>not subject to what children wanted</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"some families today, children get a vote - not in the Roman world\"</li><li class=\"d0\">If God is our <b>Father</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no <b>magical ritual</b> in a temple to get him to do what we want</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>he tells us</b> what he wants - we are to be <b>obedient</b></li></ul>"},{"t":790.05,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 17:30 - Times of ignorance, now repent","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"These times of ignorance God overlooked, but now commands all men everywhere to repent\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Second piece: not only a <b>little knowledge of God</b> - also a period of <b>ignorance</b> with sins overlooked</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans</b>: where there is <b>no law</b>, sins not reckoned</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Torah</b> revealed to <b>Israel</b> → accountable in all its details</li><li class=\"d1\">not given to the <b>Athenians</b> → accountable for <b>what they had</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now Paul brings the <b>gospel</b>; people called back starting with <b>Pentecost</b></li><li class=\"d1\">now accountable → must <b>repent</b>, return to God</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>held responsible</b> for response to the gospel, given to everyone</li></ul>"},{"t":873.02,"lv":3,"h":"Repent = metanoia","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Repent</b> = Greek <i>metanoia</i> = <b>change your mind</b></li><li class=\"d0\">They may have heard it <b>intellectually</b>: turning from error to truth - \"not totally off\"</li><li class=\"d1\">wouldn't have the <b>moral dimension</b> perfectly</li><li class=\"d0\">Before: worshiped other gods, God <b>blessed</b> you despite it</li><li class=\"d1\">now hearing the truth → <b>responsible to believe and follow</b></li><li class=\"d1\">turn back to the old things → \"God's not going to <b>overlook</b> or excuse it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":936.63,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 17:31 - The appointed day and the resurrection","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Appointed a day</b> to <b>judge the world in righteousness</b> by the <b>man whom he has ordained</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Assurance to all by raising him from the dead</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ending on <b>Jesus</b>: a time coming when everyone gives an <b>account</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hence <b>repent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus = the <b>judge</b> - proved by <b>raising him from the dead</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Answers original question: <b>who's Jesus? who's Anastasis?</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus = man returning to <b>judge</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Anastasis = him <b>raised from the dead</b> - the <b>sign</b> it's true</li></ul>"},{"t":999.54,"lv":3,"h":"What the gospel is","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We leave this out: in <b>America</b>, conditioned by <b>Protestantism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">gospel = \"telling people how to get saved\" - <b>not biblically</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Biblically the gospel = what Jesus did</b> - \"the good news\"</li><li class=\"d1\">in Acts: who Jesus is and what he did → people respond <b>\"What must I do to be saved?\"</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1049.44,"lv":3,"h":"Saved from what?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Saved from what? Paul <b>not</b> running down a list of sins</li><li class=\"d1\">\"do you drink, smoke, dance the hoochie cooch\" - no</li><li class=\"d1\">not saved from <b>sins committed</b> - from <b>death</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Last thing we say of Christ: <b>returning to judge the living and the dead</b></li><li class=\"d1\">includes <b>wrath</b> - but not only: also <b>reward</b> for the faithful and righteous</li><li class=\"d0\">Tell someone all Christ has done: <b>rejected, crucified, rose, ascended, enthroned</b>, ruling creation, will <b>return to judge</b> - including you</li><li class=\"d1\">next question: <b>\"What must I do to be saved?\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= how to prepare to <b>stand before Christ</b> and give account</li><li class=\"d0\">Why this is the major emphasis - the last bit \"throws them all\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>core of the gospel</b>: raised, coming to judge → <b>repent and prepare</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not negotiable</b> - can't stop at \"God loves us all, close to all of us, all his children\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1191.41,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's method: bring them along","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Long sermon with nothing they can't <b>accept</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Then <b>historical event</b> of Christ's death and resurrection - outside their prepared ideas</li><li class=\"d0\">Same as the <b>Jewish-context sermons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">start with <b>Abraham</b>, the <b>law given to Moses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then Jesus is the <b>Messiah</b>, <b>crucified</b> - \"they all go, huh?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Method: \"You agree with me about this?\" → take it to its <b>natural conclusion</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some, like the <b>Bereans</b>, go to the scriptures and <b>accept</b></li><li class=\"d1\">others \"jump off board\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Here a little different - further back: <b>Babel</b>, when Gentiles and Jews were one</li></ul>"},{"t":1318.8,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 17:32 - Some mocked: why resurrection offends","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>When they heard of the resurrection of the dead, some mocked</b></li><li class=\"d1\">reject it and think it <b>ridiculous</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why: <b>Plato</b> - the body is the <b>prison house of the soul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Stoics want <b>universals</b>, not particulars</li><li class=\"d1\">body: tires, eats, <b>bathroom</b> - a hindrance</li><li class=\"d1\">\"why would you want to <b>come back to life</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Epicureans</b>: life of the <b>intellect</b></li><li class=\"d1\">soul free → <b>pure contemplation</b> without ceasing</li><li class=\"d0\">Same as many <b>contemporary Christians</b>: die, escape, <b>\"I'll fly away\"</b> - resurrection of the body \"lost someplace\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1422.78,"lv":3,"h":"N. T. Wright and escapist religions","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>N. T. Wright</b> (retired Anglican Bishop of Durham, NT scholar)</li><li class=\"d1\">not interested in life after death - interested in <b>life after life after death</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>new heavens and new earth</b>, <b>Christ returns, we are resurrected</b> - \"the thrust\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Common in <b>non-Christian religions</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Buddhism</b>: escaping; existence is suffering; <b>cease to exist</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Hinduism</b>: a <b>wheel</b> you're trapped in</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Christianity</b>: <b>not redeeming us out of the world - redeeming the world</b></li><li class=\"d1\">his <b>creation</b>, all of it, brought back to himself</li></ul>"},{"t":1527.0,"lv":3,"h":"Protestant and medieval parallels; Gnostic eschatology","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Very <b>Protestant</b> way of looking: heaven on a cloud with a harp, <b>no bodies</b></li><li class=\"d1\">listener raised that way: body \"tempts you to sin\", only physical, <b>spiritual</b> is what matters</li><li class=\"d1\">actually <b>ancient Greek</b> - they'd say <b>mental</b> instead of spiritual</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Medieval Roman Catholicism</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Aquinas</b> affirms resurrection of body, but spends much time on the <b>beatific vision</b> outside the body</li><li class=\"d1\">how the two fit \"never sort of explained\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: how much do the <b>Gnostic heresies</b> figure in?</li><li class=\"d1\">not directly - for Aquinas it's <b>Platonism</b>, via Greek philosophy</li><li class=\"d1\">Protestant heaven-or-hell, body forgotten = \"basically a <b>Gnostic eschatology</b>\" - not from reading Gnostic Gospels</li></ul>"},{"t":1683.42,"lv":3,"h":"Harold Bloom: The American Religion","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Harold Bloom</b>, Shakespeare professor at <b>Yale</b> - book <i>The American Religion</i></li><li class=\"d1\">one American religion; best examples: <b>Southern Baptist Convention</b> and <b>Mormons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">argues not Christianity but <b>Gnosticism</b></li><li class=\"d0\">About <b>denying the world</b> in morals and ethics</li><li class=\"d1\">pleasurable things - food, drink, tobacco, alcohol - <b>evil</b>, pushed away</li><li class=\"d1\">everything about <b>going to heaven</b>, leaving body and world behind</li><li class=\"d0\">Threads from the <b>Second Great Awakening</b> (19th c.) through US religious experience</li><li class=\"d0\">Bloom considered <b>himself a Gnostic</b>; had read the <b>Gnostic Gospels</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1814.44,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 17:32-34 - Hear again; Dionysius and Damaris","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Some mock outright; others: <b>\"We will hear you again on this matter\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">maybe considering converting - or Athenians \"just really like hearing new things\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Paul departed from among them</b>; some <b>joined him and believed</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Dionysius the Areopagite</b>, a woman named <b>Damaris</b>, others</li><li class=\"d0\">Tradition: <b>Dionysius</b> = first <b>bishop of Athens</b></li><li class=\"d1\">named prominently, one of only two named - at minimum a <b>prominent member</b> of early community</li></ul>"},{"t":1901.78,"lv":2,"h":"The Dionysian writings","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Brief detour: works attributed to <b>Dionysius the Areopagite</b></li><li class=\"d1\">believed for most of Christian history to be by him</li><li class=\"d1\">most scholars now <b>reject</b> that</li><li class=\"d0\">Reasons</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>nobody mentions them</b> before <b>5th/6th c.</b>; 6th c. suddenly quoted</li><li class=\"d1\">we have a <b>tiny fraction</b> of what was written - can't be sure</li><li class=\"d1\">books say written in <b>6th c. Syria</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Scholar's line: two things certain - <b>not 6th century, not Syria</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"the only things we know for sure is that that's wrong\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Still very <b>influential</b>, authoritative <b>East and West</b> from 6th c. on</li><li class=\"d1\">maybe a <b>later saint named after him</b> - never prove; \"until the resurrection\"</li><li class=\"d1\">either way Dionysius = important <b>saint</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2092.61,"lv":3,"h":"Reading Dionysius","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not long, <b>not easy</b> - \"read a paragraph and chew on it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"read it once and was kind of exhausting\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Most famous: <b><i>Celestial Hierarchies</i></b> - angels, <b>cherubim, seraphim, thrones, dominions, powers, archangels</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b><i>Ecclesiastical Hierarchies</i></b> - bishops, priests, deacons</li><li class=\"d1\">draws connections between <b>angelic realm</b> and <b>church</b> structure</li><li class=\"d0\">Name tangents: people take names of the past</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Dennis</b> from <b>Dionysius</b>; <b>Denis</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Louis</b> from <b>Elias</b> (Greek for <b>Elijah</b>), the \"E\" drops off</li></ul>"}]});
